S=/root/pe-sports/goaltrade; mkdir -p $S /root/pe-builder/runs
cat > /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_goaltrade.md <<'BRIEF'
You are a Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 12, started to run ALONGSIDE other sessions. Work only in /root/pe-sports/goaltrade (new files) and new tables in schema 'goaltrade'; read anything; do not edit /root/pe-sports/grid, the paper trader, or the goal monitor's code. Import the grid's fill rules (onetest/engine) and facts.py known-at rule rather than rewriting them. HARD RULES: paper only; never an order endpoint; nothing deleted; credentials only through /root/pe-queue/runner/creds.py; no purchases or sign-ups. Session 11 (books) is testing the existing goal signal's alerts in its Part C - read its page if it exists and do not repeat it; your job is the TRADE.
PETER'S IDEA, which is how a football trader actually works: predict that a goal is coming, take a position BEFORE it, close when the goal is scored; if the opposite happens (the other side scores, or the goal does not come in time) we know, and we get out. Build exactly that and say whether it pays.
THE ARITHMETIC TO KEEP IN FRONT OF YOU (measure it from our own tape, do not assume): backing 'a goal' (over the next half-goal line, or lay under) costs a known amount for every minute with no goal (the price drifts) and gains a known jump when a goal comes. From the recorded prices, for each minute of play and each line, measure: the average drift cost of holding N minutes with no goal, the average gain when a goal comes within N minutes, the gap paid in and out, and so the BREAK-EVEN goal probability for N = 5, 10, 15, 20. Compare with the actual base rate of a goal in that window. That gives the number that matters: how many points above the base rate a predictor must be for the trade to pay. Put that table at the top of the page.
STEP 1 - THE PREDICTOR, from everything we hold, using only what was known at the moment (facts.py known-at rule, plus each feed's measured delay): minute, score, pre-match strength (the pre-match prices themselves: favourite, expected goals implied by the over/under line), shots, shots on target, dangerous attacks, corners, expected goals in the last 5/10/15 minutes and since kick-off, each side separately; red cards where held; AND the market's own signals, which arrive instantly and cover every match: the over/under and match-odds prices and how they moved in the last 1/3/5 minutes relative to normal time decay (the price refusing to drift = money expecting a goal), money traded per minute, money waiting on each side, the gap. Target: a goal in the next 5/10/15 minutes, and WHICH side scores. Fit on the first half of the dates only; simple, readable models first (a few conditions; then a logistic model); report on the second half: predicted against actual goal rate in ten bands, and the uplift of the top band over the base rate for that minute and score. Do the same using the existing goal monitor's alerts as one input, and say whether it adds anything to the rest.
STEP 2 - THE TRADES, through the real fill rules (take the price on offer, money shown, £50 cap, 2% commission, prices at least as late as our information), each as its own row with both halves:
 (a) goal coming, side unknown: BACK the over on the lowest line still open (back side first; show lay-under separately and only where the lay price is under 3.0), close on the first fresh price after the market reopens following the goal; stop = out after N minutes with no goal or when the position is down X% of the stake (5, 10, 20), whichever first;
 (b) goal coming for a named side: BACK that side in match odds (if level or behind) or back it on the next-goal/handicap market if recorded; close after their goal; if the OTHER side scores, out at the first price after the reopening (measure what that costs - it is the real risk of this trade and must be shown as the worst-case line);
 (c) no goal coming (the predictor's bottom band): the mirror with small risk - back the under for N minutes and close, stop if a goal comes (show the size of that loss);
 (d) the same three taking a bookmaker's price instead of Betfair's where session 11's live table has one (the stale-price version), if any data exists yet.
 For each: orders, share that hit the target / the time stop / the loss stop / the opposite goal, £ kept per £100 on each half, per day, worst day, and the result without the best day.
STEP 3 - VERDICT, first line of the page: does the goal trade pay after costs on unseen days - YES / NOT YET / NO - with the uplift the predictor achieves against the uplift the arithmetic needs. If YES in any form, start it on paper now as a live paper trader of its own (plain loop, in watch.list, writing to goaltrade.paper and to reports/goal_trades_today.md: time, match, minute and score, what was predicted, bet, price, exit and why, profit) for tonight's and tomorrow's matches. If NOT YET, say exactly what is missing (faster stats, more matches with stats, a market we do not record such as next goal) and what each would add.
WRITE /root/pe-queue/reports/goal_trade.md, plain English, one screen, verdict first. Push a first version within 2 hours even if incomplete, and again at the end: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/goal_trade.md reports/goal_trades_today.md 2>/dev/null; git commit -qm "the goal trade" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Then end the session.
BRIEF
cat > /root/pe-builder/run_goaltrade.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M)
cd /root/pe-sports/goaltrade && timeout -k 60 21600 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_goaltrade.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 600 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_goaltrade.log 2>&1
echo "goaltrade session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
